[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router16
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRRouting (OSPF) on router16, which is the campus-to-backbone transit router for mswitch0.

Mechanism: Campus PCs can reach their SVI gateways on mswitch0 (local L3 SVIs on vlan10/20/30), but any route beyond must traverse router16 over 10.2.2.0/24 and then to the backbone on 172.30.1.0/24. With OSPF down on router16, routes between Campus 1 and the backbone are not exchanged, so traffic past the gateway blackholes or times out. This presents to users as “intermittent” while OSPF routes/adjacency on mswitch0 age out and briefly flap.

Localization: 
- On router16: OSPF is not running and FRR is inactive; only connected routes exist.
  Evidence: “ospfd is not running”; systemctl frr: “Active: inactive (dead)”; /etc/frr/frr.conf: “No such file or directory”; ip route shows only 10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24.
- On mswitch0: OSPF is configured and shows neighbor 10.2.2.1 (RID 172.30.1.1) with a nearly expired dead timer, indicating the peer has stopped sending hellos.
- On router7 (backbone side): No OSPF neighbor on 172.30.1.2 toward 172.30.1.1, confirming the break at router16.

Root cause: FRR/OSPF on router16 was left stopped and its configuration file missing after maintenance.

Corrective fix: Restore /etc/frr/frr.conf on router16 with OSPF enabled for 10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24, enable and start frr (systemctl enable --now frr). Verify OSPF neighbors: mswitch0↔router16 (10.2.2.0/24) and router7↔router16 (172.30.1.0/24) reach Full, and that Campus 1 routes are present on the backbone. === END ===